!FFmpeg - FFmpeg 5.1.10 (LTS) for RISC OS                    riscos1
===================================================================

FFmpeg converts, plays and inspects audio and video. This is FFmpeg
5.1.10, the current release of the 5.1 long-term-support series, built
for ARMv7 machines with NEON: Raspberry Pi 2, 3, 4 and 400, Titanium,
IGEPv5, iMX6 boards, BeagleBoard-xM, Pandaboard. It does not run on a
Pi 1 / Zero or older machines.

It contains three commands:

  ffmpeg    convert between formats, encode, cut, resize, ...
  ffprobe   show what is in a file
  ffplay    play a file in a desktop window (or full screen)

Getting started
---------------
Let the Filer see !FFmpeg (open the directory it is in), or double-click
it once. That sets up the commands. Then open a TaskWindow (Ctrl-F12):

  ffprobe SDFS::RISCOSpi.$.Videos.holiday/mp4
  ffplay  SDFS::RISCOSpi.$.Videos.holiday/mp4
  ffmpeg -i holiday/mp4 -c:v libx264 -crf 23 -c:a aac small/mp4
  ffmpeg -i song/flac -c:a libmp3lame -b:a 192k song/mp3

File names can be RISC OS style (ADFS::HardDisc4.$.Films.clip/mkv,
<Obey$Dir>.clip/mp4) or Unix style (clip.mp4). The extension after "/"
or "." picks the output format. New files get their filetype from
MimeMap.

ffplay keys: Q or Escape quit, F full screen, P or Space pause,
Left/Right seek 10 s, Up/Down seek 1 min, M mute, 9/0 volume,
W cycle video/waveform/spectrum, S step one frame.
ffplay plays sound through SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager
(John Duffell's ssb.zip, from the RDPClient page at orac.co.uk) when they
are loaded.

What is in it
-------------
Decoders for almost everything FFmpeg knows (H.264, HEVC, VP8, VP9, AV1
via dav1d, MPEG-1/2/4, ProRes, AAC, MP3, AC-3, Opus, Vorbis, FLAC, ...).
Encoders: H.264 (x264), MP3 (LAME), Opus, Vorbis, AAC, MPEG-4, MPEG-2,
MJPEG, FLAC, ProRes, FFV1 and more. Network: http, tcp, udp, rtp, rtmp,
hls (no https: there is no TLS library in this build).

Speed
-----
Most of FFmpeg's speed on ARM comes from its hand-written NEON assembly
(and dav1d's and x264's). The PackMan FFmpeg (4.4) is built for ARMv4
without VFP or NEON; this one uses all of it.

RISC OS runs with alignment checking on, and much of that assembly
assumed unaligned loads are allowed (as on Linux). For this build every
such instruction was rewritten so it cannot fault, and FFmpeg's, dav1d's
and x264's own test suites (about 3,000 checks) plus 110 decode/encode
tests all pass under an emulator that traps like RISC OS.

"Bench" (in this directory) measures the speed on your machine: double-
click it. It compares the NEON code with plain C.

Why 5.1 and not 7.x/8.x: 5.1 is the last FFmpeg with NEON code for the
AAC, AC-3, Vorbis and WMA audio decoders on 32-bit ARM (6.0 replaced it
with generic C), and the last whose ffmpeg command doesn't need threads
(RISC OS has one core). It is still maintained (LTS).

Memory
------
Each program needs about 32MB of application space (the Boot file sets
it). Memory for pictures and sound comes from a dynamic area called
"FFmpeg Heap" (up to 512MB), which goes away when the program quits.

Needs
-----
SharedUnixLibrary 1.16 or later and ARMEABISupport (both in PackMan).

Known limits
------------
- ffplay has no desktop front end: start it from a TaskWindow or Obey
  file. It uses the whole CPU while it plays.
- If ffmpeg seems to wait for a key in a TaskWindow, add -nostdin.
- No https (no TLS library), no hardware decoding.

